  "claim": "This high school located in the Bronzeville neighborhood was named after a settler who was arrested by the British military.",
  "evidencia": "Page: DuSable High School. Sentence: Jean Baptiste Point DuSable High School was a public 4–year high school located in the Bronzeville neighborhood on the South Side of Chicago, Illinois, United States.\nPage: DuSable High School. Sentence: The school was named after Chicago's first permanent non-native settler, Jean Baptiste Point Du Sable.\nPage: Jean Baptiste Point du Sable. Sentence: Jean Baptiste Point du Sable (or Point de Sable, Point au Sable, Point Sable, Pointe DuSable) (before 1750 – August 28, 1818) is regarded as the first permanent resident of what became Chicago, Illinois.\nPage: Jean Baptiste Point du Sable. Sentence: In 1779, he was living on the site of present-day Michigan City, Indiana, when he was arrested by the British military on suspicion of being an American sympathizer in the American Revolutionary War.",
