        "original_id": "d37cb823-8b7f-5c9d-accc-d327fd6443ed",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: DuSable High School\n- People: Jean Baptiste Point du Sable\n- Places: Bronzeville, Chicago, Illinois, Michigan City, Indiana\n- Topics: school naming, early Chicago history, American Revolutionary War\n\nFacts:\n- In 1779, Jean Baptiste Point du Sable was living at the site of present-day Michigan City, Indiana, when the British military arrested him on suspicion of being an American sympathizer in the American Revolutionary War.\n- Jean Baptiste Point du Sable, who lived before 1750 to August 28, 1818, is regarded as the first permanent resident of what became Chicago, Illinois.\n- DuSable High School was a public four-year high school located in the Bronzeville neighborhood on the South Side of Chicago, Illinois, United States.\n- DuSable High School was named after Jean Baptiste Point du Sable, who is described as Chicago's first permanent non-native settler.",
